    "\n",
    "### **Using data to improve FPL player scouting**\n",
    "\n",
    "I developed a deeper FPL scouting workflow to look beyond total points and evaluate players using form, minutes, expected involvement, efficiency, price, ownership and upcoming fixtures.\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis produced several clear findings:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Bruno Fernandes** is the strongest overall performer in the sample, combining 25 points, a form rating of 12.5, 12.5 points per 90 minutes and 2.84 attacking involvement.\n",
    "- **Cherki** has the highest points-per-90 rate at 18.33 and strong attacking output, but his 108 minutes means the result should be treated as an early signal rather than a guaranteed trend.\n",
    "- **João Pedro** is the leading forward, with 20 points, four attacking returns and a goal-threat score of 3.86.\n",
    "- **Ajayi** is one of the strongest budget options, producing 20 points at £4.1m and 4.88 points per price unit.\n",
    "- **Calafiori, White, Ajayi and De Cuyper** stand out among defenders through their combination of clean sheets, attacking returns, bonus points and points per 90.\n",
    "- **Tzolakis** is the leading goalkeeper in this sample, recording 20 points, two clean sheets and 10 points per 90 at £4.6m.\n",
    "\n",
    "The expected-goals and expected-assists visualization adds important context to the actual returns. Bruno Fernandes leads the sample for attacking involvement, while players such as Mbeumo and Thiago show strong expected-goal numbers without equivalent goals so far. These players may be worth monitoring, although expected returns do not guarantee future points.\n",
    "\n",
    "The value-and-ownership visualization highlights the difference between popular premium assets and potential differential picks. High-value players with relatively low ownership can offer opportunities for managers seeking an advantage, but only when their minutes and fixtures remain favourable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Fixture difficulty is also important. Liverpool has the most favourable upcoming schedule in the current sample, followed by teams such as Aston Villa, Brighton, Crystal Palace, Leeds, Manchester City and Newcastle. This increases the appeal of players from those teams, particularly when their individual statistics are already strong.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main lesson is that no single metric should determine a transfer decision. The strongest candidates combine reliable minutes, sustainable underlying numbers, acceptable price, current form and favourable fixtures. Recent points and ownership provide useful context, but they should not be used in isolation.\n",
